# Recipe 3.4: A storage account with the shutters down

**Goal:** storage that can't leak publicly, shared only through a short-lived read-only link.

**Environment:** `rg-intern-lab`, storage account `1ststorage`, container `test`, blob `hello.txt` (test text only).

**Status:** complete.

---

## The idea in plain English

- A **storage account** holds files (blobs). Misconfigured storage is a classic breach source, because one wrong setting can make files readable by anyone on the internet.
- I created the account with the dangerous options **off from the start**.
- To share a file, I used a **SAS** (shared access signature): a link that grants one permission, on one file, until a set time. It is a temporary pass, not a permanent key.
- I used a **user-delegation SAS**, signed with my Entra sign-in. The alternative is signing with an **account key**, which is an all-powerful password for the whole account.

---

## What I did

### 1. Create the account with the shutters down

```powershell
$SA = "1ststorage"
az storage account create -g rg-intern-lab -n $SA -l southafricanorth --sku Standard_LRS --allow-blob-public-access false --min-tls-version TLS1_2 --https-only true
```

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--sku Standard_LRS` | Cheapest redundancy: three copies in one datacentre |
| `--allow-blob-public-access false` | Nobody can make a container public, even later |
| `--min-tls-version TLS1_2` | Rejects older, weaker encryption |
| `--https-only true` | Rejects plain HTTP |

Storage account names are lowercase letters and numbers only, 3 to 24 characters, and unique across Azure.

**Verify:**

```powershell
az storage account show -n $SA -g rg-intern-lab --query "{PublicBlob:allowBlobPublicAccess, MinTLS:minimumTlsVersion, HttpsOnly:enableHttpsTrafficOnly}" -o table
```

Result: `PublicBlob False`, `MinTLS TLS1_2`, `HttpsOnly True`.

### 2. Give myself a data role

```powershell
$SA_ID = az storage account show -n $SA -g rg-intern-lab --query id -o tsv
$ME = az ad signed-in-user show --query id -o tsv
az role assignment create --role "Storage Blob Data Contributor" --assignee-object-id $ME --assignee-principal-type User --scope $SA_ID
```

- `$SA_ID` is the account's resource ID, used as the **scope** (where the permission applies).
- `$ME` is my user's object ID in Entra.
- Owner manages the account itself (management plane) but cannot read or write the blobs inside it (data plane). `Storage Blob Data Contributor` is the data-plane role.

### 3. Create a container and upload a test file

```powershell
az storage container create --account-name $SA -n test --auth-mode login
Set-Content -Path "$env:TEMP\hello.txt" -Value "hello from recipe 3.4"
az storage blob upload --account-name $SA -c test -n hello.txt -f "$env:TEMP\hello.txt" --auth-mode login
```

- `--auth-mode login` uses my Entra sign-in and role. **No account key was used.**
- The test file was in my temp folder, so it could not be committed to Git by accident. I deleted it afterwards with `Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\hello.txt"`.

Result: the container was created (`"created": true`) and the upload succeeded with `"request_server_encrypted": true`, meaning Azure encrypted the file at rest.

### 4. Prove the blob is not public

```powershell
$URL = "https://$SA.blob.core.windows.net/test/hello.txt"
curl.exe -s -o NUL -w "%{http_code}" $URL
```

- `curl.exe` (with `.exe`) is the real curl. Plain `curl` in PowerShell is a different command.
- `-s` is silent, `-o NUL` throws away the body, and `-w "%{http_code}"` prints only the HTTP status code.

Result: `409`. The anonymous request was refused, so the blob URL alone does not work. The point is that it is not `200`.

### 5. Share it with a 10-minute read-only link

```powershell
$EXP = (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().AddMinutes(10).ToString("yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm'Z'")
$SAS_URL = az storage blob generate-sas --account-name $SA -c test -n hello.txt --permissions r --expiry $EXP --auth-mode login --as-user --full-uri -o tsv
curl.exe -s $SAS_URL
```

| Part | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `$EXP` | Current UTC time plus 10 minutes (a SAS expiry is in UTC) |
| `--permissions r` | Read only |
| `--as-user` | Signed with my Entra identity (user delegation), not the account key |
| `--full-uri` | Prints the complete, ready-to-open URL |

Result: the link printed `hello from recipe 3.4`.

### 6. Watch it expire

After the expiry time had passed, I ran the same request again:

```powershell
curl.exe -s $SAS_URL
```

Result: `AuthenticationFailed`. Azure's detail said the signed expiry time (15:31 UTC) must be after the signed start time (15:38 UTC). In plain terms, the link had expired and Azure refused it. The file was no longer served.

Then I cleared the variable so the old link did not stay in my session:

```powershell
Remove-Variable SAS_URL
```

---

## Checklist: storage settings to check on every client account

| Setting | What I want | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Blob public access | Disabled | Stops anyone making a container public |
| Secure transfer (HTTPS only) | Enabled | No plain HTTP |
| Minimum TLS version | 1.2 or higher | No weak encryption |
| Shared key (account key) access | Disabled where possible | Account keys are all-powerful passwords. Use Entra roles instead |
| Network access | Restricted to a private endpoint or known IP ranges | `defaultAction` was `Allow` on mine, so any network could reach it (sign-in still required) |
| Sharing method | User-delegation SAS, read-only, short expiry | A leaked link then works briefly and for one file |
| Role assignments | Data-plane roles scoped to this account | No broad Owner or Contributor for data access |
| Redundancy | Chosen on purpose (LRS, ZRS, GRS) | Matches how much data loss the client can accept |
| Tags | `owner`, `environment`, `client` | Shows who owns it |
| Logging | Diagnostic logs sent to a workspace | Lets me see who accessed what |

On my account, `allowSharedKeyAccess` was `null` (not explicitly set) and `networkRuleSet.defaultAction` was `Allow`. I did not change them for this recipe, but both go on the checklist for real accounts.

---

## What I learned

- Turning off public access at creation is safer than fixing it later.
- Owner cannot read blobs by default. Data access needs a data-plane role, the same management-plane versus data-plane split as in Recipe 3.3.
- A user-delegation SAS is better than an account-key SAS, because it is tied to an identity and a role, and no all-powerful key is involved.
- A SAS link is a secret while it is valid, so I never paste it into chat, notes or Git.
- A short expiry limits the damage if a link leaks.

## Taste test

- [x] The SAS link worked
- [x] The SAS link stopped working after expiry (`AuthenticationFailed`)
- [x] The blob URL without a SAS returned an error (`409`)

## Not done

- Stretch (private endpoint into the VM's VNet): skipped, because it adds cost and time and the recipe does not require it.

> Never put a SAS link, an account key or my subscription ID in this file.